From b0ba2e2838cb225ea0e70d8c8500ada8c8e84722 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunrisepeak <speakshen@163.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 11:35:02 +0900
Subject: [clangd] Prerequisite modules compile in supervised worker processes

Module compilation can run in an isolated worker that receives the request's captured inputs and drafts through an internal protocol. Workers stop when their parent dies, are admitted against a shared checked memory budget and address-space limit, have bounded output and diagnostics, and leave units that are collected only after both owners release their leases. On Linux this is the default for prerequisite builds; in-process fixtures choose their policy explicitly.

* Add an internal module compiler worker request protocol

  Serialize finalized CC1 arguments and explicit captured filesystem inputs, using binary snapshot files for main contents and ordered YAML overlays. Unsupported filesystem export is the default and process-local remapped buffers are rejected. The internal clangd worker mode reconstructs the invocation and request view before compiling a staged BMI, and discards failed output.

  This foundation is not enabled in ModulesBuilder. The parent must still supervise cancellation and deadlines, terminate and reap the specific child, retain prerequisite read leases, capture the entire filesystem view, and clean up killed-worker outputs and caches. It provides no hard runtime or RSS guarantee by itself.

* Supervise isolated module compilation and capture request drafts

* Stop isolated module workers when their parent owner dies

* clangd: add explicit shared cgroup memory admission for module workers

* Collect orphan module worker units only after both owners release leases

* Apply checked address-space limits before worker frontend allocation

* Supervise prerequisite module compilers by default on Linux

  Finalize the invocation before exporting request overlays and explicit prerequisite paths. Keep publication locks and prerequisite leases through specific-child reap, then recheck cancellation before publishing.

  Share a deadline and per-pool address-space policy across jobs, with optional verified aggregate cgroup admission. Configuration failures reject compilation rather than falling back. Keep leased-unit maintenance on a separate bounded thread and drain workers before releasing their shared budget.

* Preserve relative compiler inputs and explicit in-process fixture policy

  Resolve the captured main path against its working directory while keeping the original cc1 source spelling and rejecting a different captured main. Existing compiler-hook unit fixtures explicitly select in-process execution and restore the prior policy; real LSP coverage separately exercises production worker defaults.

* Collect verified orphan module worker memory budgets under owner leases

  Consolidated patch43 includes original aa84f27 and retry/retirement follow-up aaf00bb. Revalidate actual group identity before every name-based removal retry; preserve locked leases through sidecar retirement.

* Bound Linux module worker diagnostics with owned nonblocking FIFOs

  Redirect worker stderr into a private FIFO instead of an unbounded regular file. The specific-child supervisor drains at most sixteen 4 KiB reads per poll, retains a 64 KiB prefix and discards excess bytes. Read, cancellation and timeout failures reap the actual child before closing the transport and deleting its unit. Other platforms retain their existing bounded parent read and require separate disk-bound qualification.

* Bound worker output payloads and captured request inputs before writes

  Compiler output budgets cover reduced BMI, implicit cache, global index and configured output backends. Captured inputs are checked before snapshot payload writes. Resource-ceiling requests use protocol version 2 so older workers cannot ignore the bound. These optional limits do not claim aggregate cache or filesystem quota enforcement.
